\section PhysicsEngineIntro Introduction

This plug-in simulates rigid-body dynamics with the <a href="https://mujoco.org">MuJoCo</a> physics engine. The engine
itself knows nothing about plants: it simulates articulations, which are trees of rigid links connected by joints,
and kinematic bodies that follow prescribed motion. A separate adapter converts plants built by the plant architecture
plug-in to articulations, so that their mechanical response to whatever pushes through them can be simulated.

The plug-in is deliberately specific to MuJoCo rather than a generic interface over physics engines.
PhysicsEngine::getMuJoCoModel() and PhysicsEngine::getMuJoCoData() give direct access to the compiled model and the
state, for anything the class does not wrap. Include <tt><mujoco/mujoco.h></tt> to use them.

The MuJoCo state is the source of truth. The Context is only a copy of it for rendering and other plug-ins, updated
when PhysicsEngine::updateContextGeometry() is called, so a simulation that does not render never pays for it.

PhysicsEngine::updateContextGeometry() touches only geometry whose body has moved. When rendering every frame of a large scene with little moving, call Context::markGeometryClean() after each frame, so that the Visualizer finds what moved from the Context's dirty flags rather than checking every primitive. This took a 40,000-primitive scene from about 28 ms to about 10 ms per frame.

\section PhysicsEngineDepends Installing MuJoCo

MuJoCo is distributed under the Apache 2.0 license. The plug-in is written against MuJoCo 3.16.0, whose C API changes
between releases, so that exact version is required.

<ul>
  <li>If an installed MuJoCo is found (pass <tt>-Dmujoco_DIR=&lt;prefix&gt;/lib/cmake/mujoco</tt> to CMake), it is used.</li>
  <li>Otherwise MuJoCo is downloaded from GitHub and built along with the project. This adds about a minute to the first
  build. Configure with <tt>-DHELIOS_MUJOCO_FETCH=OFF</tt> to require an installed MuJoCo instead.</li>
</ul>

\section PhysicsEngineArticulations Articulations

An articulation (\ref PhysicsArticulation) is a list of links (\ref PhysicsLink), each with a mass, an inertia, collision
shapes (\ref PhysicsCollisionShape), and a joint to its parent. Link poses are given in world coordinates in the rest pose, and a
link with no parent is fixed to the world. Context geometry follows the links once it is bound to them with
PhysicsEngine::bindObjectToLink() or PhysicsEngine::bindTubeNodesToLinks().

\code{.cpp}
    Context context;

    PhysicsArticulation pendulum;
    pendulum.name = "pendulum";

    PhysicsLink pivot;
    pivot.name = "pivot";
    pivot.position = make_vec3(0, 0, 1);
    pivot.mass = 1;
    pivot.inertia_diagonal = make_vec3(1e-3, 1e-3, 1e-3);
    pendulum.links.push_back(pivot);

    PhysicsLink bob;
    bob.name = "bob";
    bob.parent_index = 0;
    bob.position = make_vec3(0.1, 0, 0.5);
    bob.mass = 1;
    bob.inertia_diagonal = make_vec3(1e-6, 1e-6, 1e-6);
    bob.joint_type = PHYSICS_JOINT_BALL;
    pendulum.links.push_back(bob);

    PhysicsEngine engine(&context);
    uint pendulumID = engine.addArticulation(pendulum);
    uint sphere = context.addSphereObject(16, bob.position, 0.05f);
    engine.bindObjectToLink(sphere, pendulumID, 1);
    engine.initialize();

    engine.step(1000);
    engine.updateContextGeometry(); // moves the sphere in the Context to the simulated pose of the bob
\endcode

\section PhysicsEngineKinematic Kinematic boxes

Kinematic boxes follow the positions and velocities they are given and push what they touch, but are not pushed back.
Always pass the box's true velocity to PhysicsEngine::setKinematicBoxPosition(): MuJoCo resolves contacts against the
velocity of the bodies, and a box that is only repositioned lets what it pushes sink into it.

\section PhysicsEngineModels Loading models

PhysicsEngine::addModelFile() loads an MJCF or URDF file, and adds a prefix to every name in it. Its geometry collides with articulations but not with itself, kinematic boxes, or other loaded models.
PhysicsEngine::addPositionServo() drives one of its joints, PhysicsEngine::addForceTorqueSensor() adds a sensor on one of its bodies, and PhysicsEngine::bindObjectToBody() makes Context geometry follow one of its bodies.
The \ref RoboticsDoc "Robotics" plug-in builds robots on these.

\section PhysicsEngineContactSensor Contact force by what is touched

A ContactSensor measures the contact force on a body of a loaded model and every body below it, such as a whole robot, split by the partner of each contact. Each articulation link is reported under its label
(PhysicsLink::label): \ref addPlantToPhysicsEngine() labels the links of a plant "wood", "petiole", "peduncle", "leaf", "fruit", or "flower" (see \ref plantLinkLabel()). The ground plane is "ground", and every
other body, such as a kinematic box or another loaded model, is "other". One link per instance can be reported as "target" instead, with ContactSensor::setTargetLink(), such as the fruit a robot is picking,
so that the force on it can be told from the force on the rest of the plant.

\code{.cpp}
    engine.initialize();
    ContactSensor contact(&engine, "robot/base_link");
    contact.setTargetLink(plant, fruit_link);
    engine.step();
    vec3 on_wood = contact.getForce("wood");
    vec3 on_anything_but_the_fruit = contact.getTotalForce({"target"});
\endcode

Create the sensor after PhysicsEngine::initialize(), and call ContactSensor::prepareInstances() after creating more instances. ContactSensor::isPenetrating() tells whether a pose placed directly touches anything.

\section PhysicsEngineDetach Detaching links

A link with the joint type \ref PHYSICS_JOINT_DETACHABLE is held rigidly to its parent until PhysicsEngine::detachLink() releases it, after which it and its descendants move freely. This is how a branch is cut or a fruit is picked.
Set PhysicsEngineParameters::ground_collisions so that a detached link comes to rest on the ground. PhysicsEngine::splitTubesAtLink() splits any tube spanning the link and its parent, so that it parts cleanly rather than stretching.

The links that may be detached have to be chosen before PhysicsEngine::initialize(), and each adds six degrees of freedom and a constraint to the model. In return, detaching needs no change to the model, so each simulation instance can detach different links, and PhysicsEngine::resetInstance() restores them.
A held link is rigid at that joint, rather than bending as a ball joint would. PlantPhysicsParameters::detachable_links chooses them for a plant.

PhysicsEngine::getDetachableLinkLoad() gives the force in the constraint holding a link. To model a connection that fails under load, such as a fruit's stem, give the link a force with PhysicsEngine::setDetachForce():
after every physics step the load is compared with it, and the link is released when the load is larger, so a peak between control steps is not missed. Each instance has its own forces, which are kept when it is reset.
PhysicsEngine::getLoadDetachmentCount() counts the links released this way since the instance was reset.

\section PhysicsEngineInstances Running many instances

One compiled model can have many simulation instances, each with entirely separate state. PhysicsEngine::createInstance()
adds one, PhysicsEngine::resetInstance() returns one to its initial state, and PhysicsEngine::stepInstances() steps
them all in parallel. Most methods take an optional instance ID, which defaults to the first instance. All instances share the model, so they
have the same bodies and joints, and PhysicsEngine::updateContextGeometry() copies the state of whichever instance is requested to the Context.

\code{.cpp}
    engine.initialize();
    while (engine.getInstanceCount() < 16) {
        engine.createInstance();
    }
    engine.stepInstances(100);
    engine.updateContextGeometry(3); // render instance 3
\endcode

A plant built by PlantArchitecture is not at rest under gravity, so it swings when simulated. To start every episode from rest, let one instance settle and call PhysicsEngine::setResetPose(): resetting any instance, and
creating one, then puts every joint where that instance's are, with no velocity.

PhysicsEngine::getDisplayBodyPoses() records the world pose of every body whose motion moves Context geometry, and PhysicsEngine::showDisplayBodyPoses() shows such a pose again by moving the geometry, for drawing
the frames of a movie after a run, possibly from poses recorded in another simulator of the same scene.

\section PhysicsEnginePlants Simulating plants

With the plant architecture plug-in in the project's list of plug-ins, \ref addPlantToPhysicsEngine() adds a plant.
Each internode, petiole, and peduncle segment of the plant becomes a capsule link, and each leaf, fruit, and flower
becomes a mass link. Links are connected to their parents by elastic ball joints whose stiffness comes from beam
bending theory (\f$K = EI/L\f$). The links and joints are the same ones written by
PlantArchitecture::writePlantStructureUSD() for IsaacSim, and are available directly from
PlantArchitecture::getPlantArticulation(), whose link indices match those of the articulation in the engine.

\code{.cpp}
    Context context;
    PlantArchitecture plantarchitecture(&context);
    plantarchitecture.loadPlantModelFromLibrary("apple");
    uint plantID = plantarchitecture.buildPlantInstanceFromLibrary(make_vec3(0, 0, 0), 175);

    PhysicsEngine engine(&context);
    addPlantToPhysicsEngine(engine, context, plantarchitecture, plantID);
    uint boxID = engine.addKinematicBox(make_vec3(-0.4, 0, 1), make_vec3(0.1, 0.1, 0.1));
    engine.initialize();

    for (int s = 0; s < 1000; s++) {
        // the box moves 1 mm per 2 ms step, i.e. at 0.5 m/s along x
        engine.setKinematicBoxPosition(boxID, make_vec3(-0.4f + 0.001f * s, 0, 1), make_vec3(0.5f, 0, 0));
        engine.step();
    }
    engine.updateContextGeometry(); // moves the plant and box geometry in the Context to their simulated poses
\endcode

PhysicsEngine::updateContextGeometry() moves the plant's Context geometry rigidly with the link it belongs to, and moves
the nodes of internode and petiole tubes, so the deflected plant can be rendered with the Visualizer or used by other
plug-ins. The PlantArchitecture model itself is not updated.

\section PhysicsEngineSpeed Simulation speed of plants

Flexible wood is by far the largest cost of simulating a plant. A fruiting-wall apple tree without leaves, with 2381 links, took 26 ms per step with flexible internodes and 0.7 ms with PlantPhysicsParameters::rigid_internodes, measured on one Apple Silicon Mac.
PlantPhysicsParameters::flexible_links keeps chosen internodes flexible while the rest are rigid, which gives the wood near where the plant is touched its compliance at little cost. PlantPhysicsParameters::rigid_leaves does the same for petioles and leaves, which then cannot be pushed aside, for when they matter only to what a camera sees.

\section PhysicsEngineIntegrator Integrator and time step

By default the simulation uses MuJoCo's <tt>discrete</tt> integrator, which treats joint stiffness implicitly. The beam
stiffness of short, thin segments is far higher than an explicit spring can be integrated at a practical time step;
with the <tt>implicitfast</tt> integrator a plant diverges unless PlantPhysicsParameters::apply_joint_armature
adds the artificial joint inertia that the USD export uses for the same reason. If the simulation diverges,
PhysicsEngine::step() throws an error rather than letting MuJoCo silently reset the state.

\section PhysicsEngineContact Contact penetration

MuJoCo contacts are soft: a penetrating contact is pushed apart over a time constant
(PhysicsEngineParameters::contact_time_constant) rather than instantly, so some overlap between a link and a
moving body is inherent to the method. With the defaults (a 4 ms time constant, the box's velocity given, and a 2 ms
time step), stems and petioles pushed by a box moving at 0.5 m/s overlap it by about 0.5 mm on average and 2-3 mm at
most. The time constant cannot be smaller than twice the time step, so for less overlap reduce both; halving them
roughly halves the overlap and doubles the run time. PhysicsEngine::getMaxKinematicBoxPenetration() reports
the overlap. Leaves are thin plates and can still overlap a box by up to about a centimeter.

The \c PGS solver (PhysicsEngineParameters::solver) is used by default because it is several times faster than
\c Newton for articulations with thousands of links; with the discrete integrator both treat joint stiffness implicitly.

\section PhysicsEngineSceneHelpers Choosing organs of a plant

\ref findPlantLinksInBox() finds the organs of one type whose rest position is in a box, such as the fruit at heights a robot can reach. \ref findBearingInternode() finds the internode an organ grows on, and
\ref findSupportingInternodes() the internodes that carry it, nearest first, which are the ones to leave flexible (PlantPhysicsParameters::flexible_links) when the rest of the wood is rigid.

\section PhysicsEngineNearestOrgan Nearest organ of a plant

NearestPlantOrgan finds the organ of one type nearest a point, such as the wood nearest a robot's gripper, which a policy can use to keep clear of it. It searches the organs where they stand in the plant's
rest pose, standing in for a map of the plant that perception would build before a robot starts work, so it does not need the simulation. Only organs with a capsule shape can be searched: internodes,
petioles, and peduncles. NearestPlantOrganResult gives the link, the offset to the nearest point on its axis, and the distance to its surface.

\section PhysicsEngineCollisions Collisions between links

By default links collide only with kinematic boxes. With PhysicsEngineParameters::articulation_collisions enabled, they
also collide with each other, within and across articulations. PhysicsArticulation::contact_exclusions lists the pairs
that never collide. For plants, \ref addPlantToPhysicsEngine() excludes segments that meet at or near the same node,
which overlap by construction; leaves and fruit are excluded only from the link they hang from.
Stepping the simulation then pushes apart organs that were built overlapping, such as a leaf blade grown through the
stem above it. PhysicsEngine::getLinkOverlapCount() and PhysicsEngine::getMaxLinkOverlapDepth() report the
overlaps that remain, measured between the collision shapes rather than the Context geometry.

\code{.cpp}
    PlantPhysicsParameters plant_params;
    plant_params.leaf_collision_shape = "ellipsoid"; // the corners of box-shaped leaves overlap their neighbors
    plant_params.rigid_internodes = true;            // only petioles, leaves, and fruit move

    PhysicsEngineParameters params;
    params.articulation_collisions = true;
    params.gravity = false;                          // the plant as built has already sagged under its weight

    PhysicsEngine engine(&context);
    addPlantToPhysicsEngine(engine, context, plantarchitecture, plantID, USDExportParameters(), plant_params);
    engine.initialize(params);
    while (engine.getSimulationTime() < 2.f) {
        engine.step(10);
        if (engine.getMaxLinkSpeed() < 0.002f) {
            break;
        }
    }
    engine.updateContextGeometry();
\endcode

A single box or ellipsoid cannot follow a folded or curved leaf blade: the blade of an apple leaf is folded along its
midrib, so a stem can clear its ellipsoid and still pass through the blade. Setting
PlantPhysicsParameters::leaf_collision_shape to \c "triangles" gives each triangle of the blade its own thin
collision prism, which follows the blade but makes each step several times more expensive. It is only practical for
leaves with few triangles: the finely triangulated leaves of a bean produce thousands of contacts, whose dense
constraint matrices exhaust the solver's memory, and PhysicsEngine::step() then reports an error.

Resolving the overlaps of a large plant this way takes far longer than growing it. A 700-link apple tree takes about
1 s to relax with rigid internodes (about 3 ms per step), compared with about 0.01 s for PlantArchitecture to grow
it by 5 days. With flexible internodes each step costs several times more, and young shoots bend by up to 20 cm to
clear the leaves along them. A leaf whose blade is pierced by a rigid stem may not be able to move clear of it.
The relaxed pose is not written back to the PlantArchitecture model, so overlaps return if the plant grows further.

Pushing organs apart also loads the plant. A fruit grown through a branch is pushed out of it by bending the branch, and the
branch then presses on the fruit, which can load the fruit's stem more than a picked fruit's stem ever is. To keep the plant
as it was built while still stopping its links from passing into each other afterward, set
PhysicsEngineParameters::keep_initial_link_overlaps: each pair of links that overlaps when the simulation is initialized
then never collides, and every other pair does. PhysicsEngine::getExcludedInitialOverlapCount() gives the number of pairs
left overlapping.

\code{.cpp}
    PhysicsEngineParameters params;
    params.articulation_collisions = true;
    params.keep_initial_link_overlaps = true;    // organs built overlapping stay as they are; nothing new can overlap
    engine.initialize(params);
\endcode

*/
